WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.420
Practice is necessary for expertise, but not all practice has the same value.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:08.245
Repeating a comfortable task can maintain familiarity without building much new

00:00:08.245 --> 00:00:08.770
capability.

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:10.660
Deliberate practice is more focused.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:15.200
It uses clear goals, concentrated effort, feedback, correction, and increasing challenge.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.565
It asks the learner to work at the edge of current ability,

00:00:17.565 --> 00:00:20.405
where improvement is possible but mistakes are still visible.

00:00:20.405 --> 00:00:23.155
Complex skills often need to be broken into components.

00:00:23.155 --> 00:00:25.200
A skilled analyst, engineer, operator,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.895
or manager performs many small judgments that are easy to miss from the outside.

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:32.720
Training can isolate those components: recognizing cues, choosing a method,

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.845
checking an assumption, explaining a tradeoff, reviewing an output,

00:00:35.845 --> 00:00:37.240
or recovering from an error.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:41.160
Once the components improve, they can be recombined into more realistic performance.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.960
Time served does not automatically produce expertise.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.610
Someone can repeat the same weak approach for years if the environment never provides

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.910
clear feedback or if mistakes are hidden by luck,

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.025
team support, or forgiving conditions.

00:00:52.025 --> 00:00:55.340
Experience becomes more valuable when it includes reflection, correction,

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:56.510
increasing complexity,

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:59.500
and enough variation to teach when the old pattern no longer applies.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.935
A useful improvement cycle is simple to describe,

00:01:01.935 --> 00:01:04.100
even when it takes discipline to execute.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.680
Define the target performance.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:06.680
Attempt the task.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.910
Compare the result against a stronger standard.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:09.895
Identify the gap.

00:01:09.895 --> 00:01:10.910
Adjust the method.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.018
Try again under a slightly different condition.

00:01:13.018 --> 00:01:15.225
Expertise grows through many cycles like this,

00:01:15.225 --> 00:01:17.520
not through a single heroic burst of effort.
